      "delta": {
        "beforeText": "A small test charge in an electric field responds differently from a small current-carrying coil in a magnetic field.\nIn an electrostatic field, a positive test charge is pushed along the tangent to an electric field line, while a negative test charge is pushed in the opposite direction.\n\nThe source's statement about force following a field line therefore needs the condition that the charge is positive.\nFor an ideal closed loop carrying a steady current, the net magnetic force is zero if the external field is static and has the same strength and direction everywhere on the loop.\n\nZero net force does not mean that the individual pieces feel no force: those forces may still produce a torque that turns the coil.\nThe circulating current determines a direction perpendicular to the coil's plane.\n\nWhen this direction is parallel or antiparallel to the magnetic field, the magnetic torque is also zero.\nThe same-direction position is stable, and the opposite-direction position is unstable.\n\nThus, the source's statement that the coil experiences only a turning torque and settles into an equilibrium position relative to a current or magnet must be understood in terms of its orientation and whether it can turn freely.\nIt does not keep turning at every orientation.",
        "afterText": "A small test charge in an electric field responds differently from a small current-carrying coil in a magnetic field.\nIn an electrostatic field, a positive test charge is pushed along the tangent to an electric field line, while a negative test charge is pushed in the opposite direction.\n\nThe source's statement about force following a field line therefore needs the condition that the charge is positive.\nFor an ideal closed loop carrying a steady current, the net magnetic force is zero if the external field is static and has the same strength and direction everywhere on the loop.\n\nZero net force does not mean that the individual pieces feel no force: those forces may still produce a torque that turns the coil.\nThe circulating current determines a direction perpendicular to the coil's plane. Curl the fingers of your right hand along the conventional current around the coil, and your extended thumb points in this perpendicular direction.\n\nWhen this direction is parallel or antiparallel to the magnetic field, the magnetic torque is also zero.\nThe same-direction position is stable, and the opposite-direction position is unstable.\n\nThus, the source's statement that the coil experiences only a turning torque and settles into an equilibrium position relative to a current or magnet must be understood in terms of its orientation and whether it can turn freely.\nIt does not keep turning at every orientation.",
        "beforeTextSha256": "46fbf7fa4b48dfcc36b361c5f7550ffe30ca8d5a972910a20ed8f586814c84a4",
        "afterTextSha256": "16687b4ba8bbe1cff850d5de17e86693f315894fe0270043a73ed436a85cdc4b",
        "exactAddedText": " Curl the fingers of your right hand along the conventional current around the coil, and your extended thumb points in this perpendicular direction.",
